1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,843
1973: The Oil Shock and the Winter

2
00:00:02,923 --> 00:00:05,675
Singapore Turned Down the Lights

3
00:00:05,675 --> 00:00:07,355
In the winter of 1973,

4
00:00:07,435 --> 00:00:12,075
Singapore's lights did not go
out — and that was not luck.

5
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:15,535
The island had no oil of
its own, yet it refined

6
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:18,928
other people's oil on a
world scale and burned

7
00:00:19,008 --> 00:00:22,475
imported oil for every
unit of its electricity.

8
00:00:22,475 --> 00:00:26,779
When the Arab oil producers cut
production and the price of crude

9
00:00:26,859 --> 00:00:31,299
quadrupled in three months,
Singapore's response was a strange one:

10
00:00:31,379 --> 00:00:35,481
it refused to help itself to the
oil sitting in its own tanks,

11
00:00:35,561 --> 00:00:40,350
taxed its own motorists hard, and
asked everyone to save a litre a day.

12
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:43,116
The lights were turned down deliberately,

13
00:00:43,196 --> 00:00:45,625
by policy — and only just a little.

14
00:00:45,625 --> 00:00:50,536
By 1973 Singapore was one of
the world's top three refining

15
00:00:50,616 --> 00:00:55,100
and trading centres, alongside
Houston and Rotterdam.

16
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,046
Four refineries had been
built in just over a decade:

17
00:00:59,126 --> 00:01:04,819
Shell's on Pulau Bukom, opened in 1961;
Esso's on Pulau Ayer Chawan in 1970,

18
00:01:04,899 --> 00:01:09,528
starting at 90,000 barrels a
day; Mobil's on the Jurong coast;

19
00:01:09,608 --> 00:01:14,237
and the Singapore Petroleum
Company's plant on Pulau Merlimau,

20
00:01:14,317 --> 00:01:18,875
which came on stream in July
1973 — months before the shock.

21
00:01:18,875 --> 00:01:22,432
Together the refineries
contributed more than

22
00:01:22,512 --> 00:01:25,825
30 percent of total manufacturing output.

23
00:01:25,825 --> 00:01:29,877
They imported crude from the
Middle East, Africa and Asia,

24
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:35,007
refined it, and shipped most of it
out again; in the course of the 1970s

25
00:01:35,087 --> 00:01:39,575
the island's refining capacity
reached a million barrels a day.

26
00:01:39,575 --> 00:01:43,300
Almost everything else
ran on the same barrel.

27
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:46,247
BP's Singapore marketing manager,

28
00:01:46,327 --> 00:01:50,742
Colin Horn, put it plainly
in 1973: "About 98% of

29
00:01:50,822 --> 00:01:54,675
our energy resources are derived from oil.

30
00:01:54,675 --> 00:01:58,500
All our electricity is generated from oil.

31
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,443
Town gas and bottle gas is
also obtained from oil...

32
00:02:02,523 --> 00:02:06,700
almost the entire economy
of the country runs on oil."

33
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,479
A country with no oil, whose
whole economy ran on it,

34
00:02:10,559 --> 00:02:13,325
was about to find out what that meant.

35
00:02:13,325 --> 00:02:18,410
When war came to the Middle East in
October 1973, the Arab oil-exporting

36
00:02:18,490 --> 00:02:22,786
countries cut production — by
a quarter at the peak measure —

37
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:27,600
and embargoed the United States,
the Netherlands and a few others.

38
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:31,000
Singapore was not a named target.

39
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,372
Its exposure came through the world
market: less crude being produced anywhere,

40
00:02:36,452 --> 00:02:39,075
and a price that climbed out of sight.

41
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:43,182
The posted price of
crude went from $3.00 a

42
00:02:43,262 --> 00:02:47,855
barrel to $5.11 within
October, and to $11.65 at

43
00:02:47,935 --> 00:02:52,431
the producers' Tehran
meeting in January 1974 —

44
00:02:52,511 --> 00:02:56,600
roughly four times higher in three months.

45
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,818
The embargo's public face was
Saudi Arabia's oil minister,

46
00:03:00,898 --> 00:03:04,670
Sheikh Ahmed Zaki Yamani,
and its instrument was the

47
00:03:04,750 --> 00:03:08,820
production cut as much as the
embargo list: a quarter of

48
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:12,975
the world's oil supply politics
had changed in a month.

49
00:03:12,975 --> 00:03:15,613
For a refining island, the question was

50
00:03:15,693 --> 00:03:18,550
not whether Singapore would be embargoed.

51
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:22,750
It was how much crude would
still arrive, and at what price.

52
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:26,897
The squeeze, as it reached
Singapore, is recorded week by

53
00:03:26,977 --> 00:03:31,500
week in the American embassy's
cables home, now declassified.

54
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:36,242
On the 8th of November 1973 the
embassy reported that the Arab

55
00:03:36,322 --> 00:03:40,598
cutbacks "will probably result
in 10 percent decrease in

56
00:03:40,678 --> 00:03:45,500
oil supplies throughout region
according to local refineries".

57
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,406
A joint Industry-Government
Committee on petroleum supply

58
00:03:49,486 --> 00:03:53,952
had been set up under the Finance
Ministry's Permanent Secretary,

59
00:03:54,032 --> 00:03:56,609
Ngiam Tong Dow, and the government was

60
00:03:56,689 --> 00:04:00,675
drawing up a contingency plan
against a serious shortage.

61
00:04:00,675 --> 00:04:04,844
The next day's cable was blunter:
the American oil companies

62
00:04:04,924 --> 00:04:11,004
expected a cutback of 10 to 20 percent in
crude supplies to Singapore-based refineries,

63
00:04:11,084 --> 00:04:14,200
though nobody could say
when it would start.

64
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:17,859
The newest refinery, SPC,
was a joint venture of Amoco,

65
00:04:17,939 --> 00:04:22,550
the Summit group and the Development
Bank of Singapore, and it had no

66
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:26,698
crude of its own — for its first
nine months it had relied on

67
00:04:26,778 --> 00:04:30,925
its partners' supply, and it
feared being cut off altogether.

68
00:04:30,925 --> 00:04:35,004
Singapore's Foreign Minister
planned to approach Kuwait and

69
00:04:35,084 --> 00:04:38,952
Saudi Arabia to ask that
Singapore be put on a "good guy

70
00:04:39,032 --> 00:04:42,970
list" and allowed full shipments,
pointing out that SPC's

71
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:47,350
products supplied Malaysia,
Bangladesh and Indonesia as well.

72
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:48,856
At the sharpest point,

73
00:04:48,936 --> 00:04:52,325
the arithmetic ran through
the Aramco partners.

74
00:04:52,325 --> 00:04:56,008
A 35 percent cutback
of Saudi crude to Exxon

75
00:04:56,088 --> 00:05:00,113
was expected to mean a 5
to 10 percent reduction

76
00:05:00,193 --> 00:05:03,700
in supplies to Esso's Singapore refinery.

77
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:08,131
Saudi Arabia was also insisting
that refineries receiving its

78
00:05:08,211 --> 00:05:12,124
crude must not supply American
military requirements —

79
00:05:12,204 --> 00:05:16,708
and Esso Singapore duly terminated
its bunkering and shipments

80
00:05:16,788 --> 00:05:21,225
to United States military
customers, pleading force majeure.

81
00:05:21,225 --> 00:05:25,231
Singapore, for its part, made
clear it did not want refined

82
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:29,675
products shipped on to embargoed
destinations through its port.

83
00:05:29,675 --> 00:05:32,681
In the embassy's shorthand,
the government "did

84
00:05:32,761 --> 00:05:35,650
not want Singapore to
be another Rotterdam".

85
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:40,675
By the 29th of November the line
had hardened into a principle.

86
00:05:40,675 --> 00:05:44,436
The government, the embassy
reported, had advised the oil

87
00:05:44,516 --> 00:05:48,681
companies to "not do a thing"
that might jeopardise Singapore's

88
00:05:48,761 --> 00:05:52,400
non-aligned status — and the
companies readily agreed.

89
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,707
The same day, Shell told the
embassy it could provide neither jet

90
00:05:56,787 --> 00:06:02,511
fuel for the aircraft of Governor Ronald
Reagan, in Singapore as a presidential envoy,

91
00:06:02,591 --> 00:06:05,818
nor any fuel for United
States military aircraft;

92
00:06:05,898 --> 00:06:10,408
some 10 to 15 itinerant aircraft a
month that landed to refuel could

93
00:06:10,488 --> 00:06:14,875
not be refuelled. (Reagan's aircraft
was, in the end, refuelled.)

94
00:06:14,875 --> 00:06:18,680
The cable added a warning
that reached beyond Singapore:

95
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:23,952
the ASEAN partners who took Singapore's
refined products "would be seriously

96
00:06:24,032 --> 00:06:28,125
affected" if Arab crude supplies
were sufficiently reduced.

97
00:06:28,125 --> 00:06:30,729
The hardest decision of that winter

98
00:06:30,809 --> 00:06:33,800
concerned the oil already in Singapore.

99
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:37,092
The refineries' tanks held
stocks that, on paper,

100
00:06:37,172 --> 00:06:40,750
could have kept the island
supplied for a long time.

101
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:44,622
Lee Kuan Yew later described
the choice in his memoirs:

102
00:06:44,702 --> 00:06:49,867
"Within days of the oil crisis in
October 1973, I decided to give a clear

103
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:54,968
signal to the oil companies that we
did not claim any special privilege

104
00:06:55,048 --> 00:06:59,575
over the stocks of oil they held
in their Singapore refineries.

105
00:06:59,575 --> 00:07:02,740
If we blocked export from
those stocks, we would have

106
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:05,740
enough oil for our own
consumption for two years,

107
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:09,800
but we would have shown ourselves
to be completely undependable."

108
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,438
The "two years" figure is
Lee's own, from his memoir;

109
00:07:13,518 --> 00:07:16,675
no contemporary document
puts a number on it.

110
00:07:16,675 --> 00:07:20,461
But the decision itself is
part of the official record,

111
00:07:20,541 --> 00:07:24,327
retold by Foreign Minister
Vivian Balakrishnan in 2018:

112
00:07:24,407 --> 00:07:30,091
the government told the oil majors "we are
not going to touch a single drop of oil

113
00:07:30,171 --> 00:07:34,600
in those oil tanks that belong
to the multinational oil majors.

114
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,038
And if we need anything, we will buy

115
00:07:37,118 --> 00:07:39,775
it from you at the full market price."

116
00:07:39,775 --> 00:07:44,505
Put the two halves of that winter
together and the position was exact:

117
00:07:44,585 --> 00:07:47,460
no special privilege
for Singapore itself —

118
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:51,525
and no help for anyone else
in evading the embargo either.

119
00:07:51,525 --> 00:07:54,577
The island would take its
share of the cut along

120
00:07:54,657 --> 00:07:58,050
with the rest of the world,
and pay the world price.

121
00:07:58,050 --> 00:08:02,450
What Singapore did not do is
as striking as what it did.

122
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:06,550
There was no rationing, and
no lowering of speed limits.

123
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:11,145
Looking back in an April 1975 survey
of Singapore's energy consumption,

124
00:08:11,225 --> 00:08:14,833
the American embassy summarised
the settled instruments:

125
00:08:14,913 --> 00:08:19,640
taxes on all petroleum products for
local use had been greatly increased,

126
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:24,315
electricity rates on an ascending
graduated scale had been introduced —

127
00:08:24,395 --> 00:08:27,213
the more you used, the
more each unit cost —

128
00:08:27,293 --> 00:08:32,100
and restrictions on the use of private
vehicles had been put into effect.

129
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:34,688
Car-pooling was strongly encouraged,

130
00:08:34,768 --> 00:08:38,400
and annual vehicle taxes
were raised three times.

131
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:41,121
The state reached for the price mechanism

132
00:08:41,201 --> 00:08:44,275
and for persuasion, and
left the pumps alone.

133
00:08:44,275 --> 00:08:48,125
The persuasion had a memorable local form.

134
00:08:48,125 --> 00:08:52,370
BP's campaign told motorists to "save

135
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:57,046
a litre a day" — worth 49.5 cents a day,

136
00:08:57,126 --> 00:09:00,750
$14.85 a month, at 1973 prices.

137
00:09:00,750 --> 00:09:04,338
Its service-station dealers
agreed to switch off their

138
00:09:04,418 --> 00:09:08,482
forecourt signs at 10 p.m., to
halve their canopy lights from

139
00:09:08,562 --> 00:09:12,423
fourteen to seven, to minimise
air-conditioning and switch

140
00:09:12,503 --> 00:09:16,375
off unused lights, and to
consider shorter opening hours.

141
00:09:16,375 --> 00:09:20,707
Posters went up at every station,
and every motorist who drove in

142
00:09:20,787 --> 00:09:25,390
received a leaflet in three languages
with fifteen fuel-saving hints:

143
00:09:25,470 --> 00:09:29,258
avoid "jack rabbit starts"
and sudden stops, and keep the

144
00:09:29,338 --> 00:09:33,750
engine in tune, which was worth
about ten percent better mileage.

145
00:09:33,750 --> 00:09:38,443
Goh Chok Tong, looking back in
2001, judged that it worked:

146
00:09:38,523 --> 00:09:42,164
"In 1973, Singaporeans
heeded the Government's

147
00:09:42,244 --> 00:09:46,450
call to cut down on the use
of oil and electricity."

148
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:50,500
The contrast with the
embargoed countries was sharp.

149
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:53,804
In the United States,
states rationed petrol by

150
00:09:53,884 --> 00:09:57,261
number plate — Oregon
first — and "No Gas" signs

151
00:09:57,341 --> 00:10:00,725
went up at stations that
had run dry by midday.

152
00:10:00,725 --> 00:10:03,344
Singapore had no queues to photograph:

153
00:10:03,424 --> 00:10:06,255
its winter was fought with tax schedules,

154
00:10:06,335 --> 00:10:10,525
tariff scales and leaflets,
not with flags and ration days.

155
00:10:10,525 --> 00:10:14,575
The post's title is, in
the end, almost literal.

156
00:10:14,575 --> 00:10:19,557
In January 1974 the Public Utilities
Board set out to convert the

157
00:10:19,637 --> 00:10:24,697
lighting of all major roads to
high-pressure sodium-vapour lamps —

158
00:10:24,777 --> 00:10:29,450
the orange lamps a generation
of Singaporeans grew up under.

159
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:33,348
More than 500 were already
installed, on Nicoll Highway,

160
00:10:33,428 --> 00:10:35,621
Maxwell Road, Orchard Boulevard,

161
00:10:35,701 --> 00:10:40,522
Stamford Road and Connaught Drive,
and the change was expected to cut

162
00:10:40,602 --> 00:10:44,225
the electricity used by
street lighting by a third.

163
00:10:44,225 --> 00:10:46,725
The lights were not switched off.

164
00:10:46,725 --> 00:10:50,205
They were changed, dimmed
by technology, as part of

165
00:10:50,285 --> 00:10:54,125
the same arithmetic that was
repricing everything else.

166
00:10:54,125 --> 00:10:56,325
None of this came free.

167
00:10:56,325 --> 00:11:00,578
In December 1973 the price
of petrol in Singapore was

168
00:11:00,658 --> 00:11:04,992
raised 9 percent and diesel
14 percent — the increase,

169
00:11:05,072 --> 00:11:09,650
the embassy noted, being
largely due to increased taxes.

170
00:11:09,650 --> 00:11:15,038
Across 1973 and 1974, Singapore
prices rose by 90 percent

171
00:11:15,118 --> 00:11:19,355
for motor gasoline, 120
percent for kerosene,

172
00:11:19,435 --> 00:11:25,575
100 percent for ships' bunkers
and 80 percent for aviation fuel.

173
00:11:25,575 --> 00:11:30,660
Total petroleum consumption fell
from 19.9 million tonnes in 1973

174
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:35,348
to 18.0 million tonnes in 1974,
a decrease of 9.4 percent —

175
00:11:35,428 --> 00:11:40,512
though the embassy's breakdown
shows where the saving really sat:

176
00:11:40,592 --> 00:11:45,995
70 percent of all petroleum consumed
in Singapore was ships' bunkers,

177
00:11:46,075 --> 00:11:51,875
5 percent was aviation fuel, and only
25 percent was domestic use at all.

178
00:11:51,875 --> 00:11:54,084
The household campaign was real,

179
00:11:54,164 --> 00:11:57,025
but it was the smaller part of the fall.

180
00:11:57,025 --> 00:12:00,800
The wider economy took
the shock through prices.

181
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:04,937
On the World Bank's consumer price series,

182
00:12:05,017 --> 00:12:10,058
Singapore's inflation went
from 2.1 percent in 1972

183
00:12:10,138 --> 00:12:15,079
to 19.6 percent in 1973
and 22.4 percent in 1974 —

184
00:12:15,159 --> 00:12:19,175
still the highest figure in that series.

185
00:12:19,175 --> 00:12:22,795
Economic growth fell from 11 percent

186
00:12:22,875 --> 00:12:27,626
in 1973 to 6.8 percent
in 1974: a hard landing,

187
00:12:27,706 --> 00:12:30,275
but not a recession year.

188
00:12:30,275 --> 00:12:34,550
Almost 17,000 workers were
retrenched during 1974,

189
00:12:34,630 --> 00:12:38,732
mainly in electronics,
textiles and woodworking,

190
00:12:38,812 --> 00:12:41,425
even as total employment rose.

191
00:12:41,425 --> 00:12:45,655
The petroleum trade balance swung from a

192
00:12:45,735 --> 00:12:50,287
surplus of 67 million
US dollars in 1973 to

193
00:12:50,367 --> 00:12:55,000
a deficit of 44 million
US dollars in 1974.

194
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,825
The crisis left its marks in odd corners.

195
00:12:58,825 --> 00:13:03,293
Plaza Singapura, the big new
shopping centre on Orchard Road,

196
00:13:03,373 --> 00:13:06,425
opened in 1974 straight into the downturn;

197
00:13:06,505 --> 00:13:11,570
tenants and shoppers stayed away,
and the bank let shop units at $1 a

198
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:15,975
month to be used as showrooms
until the economy picked up.

199
00:13:15,975 --> 00:13:19,947
At Singapore General Hospital,
a new block had been designed,

200
00:13:20,027 --> 00:13:24,066
in Lee Kuan Yew's words, "lavish
with total airconditioning" —

201
00:13:24,146 --> 00:13:27,587
when the crisis struck, the
Cabinet gave instructions

202
00:13:27,667 --> 00:13:29,912
to strip the air-conditioning down,

203
00:13:29,992 --> 00:13:34,775
because the electricity it would use
had become prohibitively expensive.

204
00:13:34,775 --> 00:13:39,899
And the same winter brought violence
to the refinery shore itself:

205
00:13:39,979 --> 00:13:45,024
on the 31st of January 1974, gunmen
attacked the Bukom complex in

206
00:13:45,104 --> 00:13:50,150
the Laju incident, a story this
blog has told in the Bukom post.

207
00:13:50,150 --> 00:13:54,750
The embargo was lifted in March 1974.

208
00:13:54,750 --> 00:13:58,458
Singapore, never a target,
had no ending of its own;

209
00:13:58,538 --> 00:14:02,325
what it had instead was a
permanent change of habit.

210
00:14:02,325 --> 00:14:07,175
A further energy conservation
campaign is listed for 1977,

211
00:14:07,255 --> 00:14:11,000
and by 1981 the government
had an Energy Unit

212
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:15,500
and an Inter-Ministry Energy
Coordinating Committee.

213
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:18,633
Conservation had stopped
being an emergency

214
00:14:18,713 --> 00:14:22,300
measure and become a
standing feature of policy.

215
00:14:22,300 --> 00:14:25,600
It was tested again within the decade.

216
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:31,657
The Iranian Revolution's
disruption of 1978 to 1979 was,

217
00:14:31,737 --> 00:14:37,136
by the measure of lost
supply, larger than 1973's,

218
00:14:37,216 --> 00:14:41,600
and oil prices rose 105 percent in 1979.

219
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:46,531
This time Singapore answered
with a named, dated campaign:

220
00:14:46,611 --> 00:14:52,400
the PUB's Save Electricity Campaign
began on the 1st of April 1979.

221
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:56,384
Within weeks the Board was
warning that consumption was still

222
00:14:56,464 --> 00:15:00,714
rising and that it would tighten
its measures if it did not fall;

223
00:15:00,794 --> 00:15:04,125
Parliament took up the
subject on the 15th of May.

224
00:15:04,125 --> 00:15:09,224
The settled approach — no subsidies,
prices allowed to rise with the market,

225
00:15:09,304 --> 00:15:13,108
penalties for wasteful use,
and a publicity campaign that

226
00:15:13,188 --> 00:15:16,651
had become a permanent
feature of Singaporean life —

227
00:15:16,731 --> 00:15:22,250
carried the island through: Singapore
still grew by more than 10 percent in 1980.

228
00:15:22,250 --> 00:15:25,976
The winter of 1973 is the
moment Singapore first

229
00:15:26,056 --> 00:15:29,306
priced its oldest weakness: an island with

